<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erasmus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1466">1466</a>–<a href="/years/1536">1536</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Desiderius Erasmus, Erasmus of Rotterdam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> Erasmus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> humanist scholar, the most famous of the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. His Greek edition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> of 1516, his satire The Praise of Folly and his many letters and editions of the Church Fathers made him a leading voice for reform within the Catholic Church. He criticised abuses but refused to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>, and his tolerant middle way influenced rulers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erasmus.jpg" alt="Erasmus" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Holbein the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Holbein-erasmus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The University of Leuven trained generations of Catholic scholars, and <b>Erasmus</b> helped to establish its College of the Three Languages in 1517.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…with its university founded in 1460 and printers such as Johann Froben, became a centre of humanism where <b>Erasmus</b> lived and Hans Holbein the Younger worked, and in the eighteenth century the Bernoulli family, Leonhard Euler and Albrecht von Haller gave the Confederacy a leading place in European…</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…began teaching in Florence in 1397; Greek scholars brought manuscripts to Italy before and after the fall of Constantinople in 1453; the printer Aldus Manutius published the Greek classics in Venice; and <b>Erasmus</b> published the Greek New Testament in 1516, an event of great importance for the Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Erasmus</b> wrote in it, as did Thomas More in Utopia.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Humanist scholars called for a return to the sources of Christianity, the Bible in its original languages and the early Church Fathers; in 1516 <b>Erasmus</b> published the Greek New Testament with notes correcting the Latin Vulgate.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…written in 1486 to introduce his 900 theses, celebrated the human freedom to rise or fall by choice; Marsilio Ficino sought to reconcile Platonic philosophy with Christian faith; and <b>Erasmus</b> called his own ideal the “philosophy of Christ”, a piety drawn from the Gospels rather than from ceremonies.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in Basel. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.92,4.48&amp;year=1511" aria-label="Rotterdam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4651 369 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Erasmus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5101 639h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5101 639h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.92,4.48&amp;year=1511">What was here in 1511? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And what is all this life but a kind of comedy, wherein men walk up and down in one another's disguises and act their respective parts, till the property-man brings them back to the attiring house.&#8221;</p><footer>The Praise of Folly &#183; tr. John Wilson, 1668 &#183; spoken by Folly in his satire &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9371/pg9371-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now if a man will weigh, as it were in a pair of balances, the commodities of war on the one side and the incommodities on the other side, he shall find that unjust peace is far better than righteous war.&#8221;</p><footer>Against War (Dulce bellum inexpertis) &#183; tr. anonymous, c. 1533 (reprinted 1907) &#183; the earliest English translation of his essay &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/39487/pg39487-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nature, or rather God, hath shaped this creature, not to war, but to friendship, not to destruction, but to health, not to wrong, but to kindness and benevolence?&#8221;</p><footer>Against War (Dulce bellum inexpertis) &#183; tr. anonymous, c. 1533 (reprinted 1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/39487/pg39487-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…neither affinity nor yet kindred doth bind the minds of men together with straiter and surer bands of amity, than doth the fellowship of them that be learned in good letters and honest studies.&#8221;</p><footer>Against War (Dulce bellum inexpertis) &#183; tr. anonymous, c. 1533 (reprinted 1907) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/39487/pg39487-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And if men would but refrain from all commerce with wisdom and give up themselves to be governed by me, they should never know what it were to be old…&#8221;</p><footer>The Praise of Folly &#183; tr. John Wilson, 1668 &#183; spoken by Folly in his satire &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9371/pg9371-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What is it when one kisses his mistress' freckle neck, another the wart on her nose? When a father shall swear his squint-eyed child is more lovely than Venus? What is this, I say, but mere folly?&#8221;</p><footer>The Praise of Folly &#183; tr. John Wilson, 1668 &#183; spoken by Folly in his satire &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9371/pg9371-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
